    "['local-address [localIP]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'bgp 100', 'parent_command': ['user-group [parameter1]'], 'root': 'user-group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nbgp 100\\npeer 11.1.1.2 connect-interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Huawei, BGP configuration starts with `bgp <AS-number>` (here AS100 from the neighbor configuration)\\n2. The `local-address` parameter in Juniper is equivalent to `connect-interface` in Huawei, specifying which interface's IP to use for the BGP session\\n3. The Huawei command references the neighbor IP (11.1.1.2) first, then specifies the interface whose address (11.1.1.1) should be used as source\", 'trans_template': 'bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'peer 11.1.1.2 connect-interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/1', 'parent_command': ['user-group [parameter1]'], 'root': 'user-group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nbgp 100\\npeer 11.1.1.2 connect-interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Huawei, BGP configuration starts with `bgp <AS-number>` (here AS100 from the neighbor configuration)\\n2. The `local-address` parameter in Juniper is equivalent to `connect-interface` in Huawei, specifying which interface's IP to use for the BGP session\\n3. The Huawei command references the neighbor IP (11.1.1.2) first, then specifies the interface whose address (11.1.1.1) should be used as source\", 'trans_template': 'peer 11.1.1.2 connect-interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/1'}]]": 1,
    "['neighbor [neighbor_ip] peer-as [peer_as_number]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'bgp 100', 'parent_command': ['user-group [parameter1]'], 'root': 'user-group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated HUAWEI command for the given Juniper BGP neighbor configuration:\\n\\n##\\nbgp 100\\npeer 11.1.1.2 as-number 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In HUAWEI devices, you first need to enter the BGP process context (bgp 100 in this case, where 100 is the local AS number). The peer configuration is then done within this context, unlike Juniper where the AS number is specified directly in the neighbor command.\", 'trans_template': 'bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'peer 11.1.1.2 as-number 100', 'parent_command': ['user-group [parameter1]'], 'root': 'user-group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated HUAWEI command for the given Juniper BGP neighbor configuration:\\n\\n##\\nbgp 100\\npeer 11.1.1.2 as-number 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In HUAWEI devices, you first need to enter the BGP process context (bgp 100 in this case, where 100 is the local AS number). The peer configuration is then done within this context, unlike Juniper where the AS number is specified directly in the neighbor command.\", 'trans_template': 'peer 11.1.1.2 as-number 100'}]]": 1,
